## Production-Ready Multithreaded Design

For production, do **not** simply make the scheduler call every table in parallel without control. The best design is:

```text
Scheduler
    |
    |-- Discovers enabled table-reader jobs
    |
    |-- Submits each table job to a bounded ThreadPoolTaskExecutor
    |
    |-- Each table job acquires a lock
    |
    |-- Reads one or more batches
    |
    |-- Processes rows
    |
    |-- Updates watermark only after success
```

Key goals:

* Read multiple Oracle tables in parallel
* Read multiple databases in parallel
* Prevent the same table from being processed by two threads at once
* Prevent overload of Oracle databases
* Keep watermark updates safe
* Support horizontal scaling later with distributed locks
* Keep processing idempotent

---

## Recommended Architecture

```text
Spring Boot App
    |
    |-- Scheduler
    |       |
    |       |-- Submits table jobs
    |
    |-- Reader Thread Pool
    |       |
    |       |-- Thread 1 -> customerDb.CUSTOMER_EVENTS
    |       |-- Thread 2 -> orderDb.ORDERS
    |       |-- Thread 3 -> billingDb.INVOICES
    |
    |-- Per-Table Lock Manager
    |
    |-- Table Polling Worker
    |
    |-- Watermark Repository
    |
    |-- DataProcessor implementations
```

For production, parallelize by table first.

Avoid multiple threads reading the **same table** unless you design partition-based reading. Reading the same table concurrently with a single watermark is dangerous.

---

## Recommended Production Rules

* One active worker per table.
* Multiple tables can run in parallel.
* Use bounded thread pools.
* Use bounded Oracle connection pools.
* Use per-database concurrency limits.
* Use composite watermarks, for example `CREATED_AT + ID`.
* Make processors idempotent.
* Use retries for transient failures.
* Use dead-letter handling for poison rows.
* Use distributed locks if multiple app instances run.
* Update watermark only after the full batch is processed successfully.
* Do not use unbounded async execution.

